abstract: "This work addresses the Perfect Awareness Problem, a combinatorial optimization task that seeks the smallest set of initial spreaders required to ensure that all nodes in a social network become aware of given information. The study proposes a heuristic based on the Iterated Local Search framework that begins with an initial solution and is subsequently improved through local search and perturbation mechanisms. The local search employs a biased sampling scheme based on the weighted random sampling with reservoir technique, restricted to high-degree candidate nodes. Two existing network preprocessing techniques, edge reduction and leaf blindness, are applied to simplify the input instances. The algorithm is evaluated on benchmark networks of different sizes and densities. Experimental results show that the proposed method outperforms existing approaches in solution quality while maintaining competitive computation times. The algorithm achieves 0.22% deviation from best-known values, obtaining solutions with the best-known value for 813 out of 840 instances, including 12 new best-known solutions that had not been previously reported in the literature. Bayesian statistical analysis demonstrates superior performance with a highest winning probability versus the literature. Finally, the algorithm is evaluated on a set of large real-world instances, obtaining the best-known value in 7 instances and yielding 4 new best-known solutions with respect to the values previously reported in the literature, demonstrating its scalability and robustness across networks of vastly different sizes and structural properties."

